 phase blockers due to their ability to induce DNA damage and activate the G2/M checkpoint. Paclitaxel, while it causes cell cycle arrest, primarily targets the mitotic phase (M phase) by stabilizing microtubules, though it can indirectly cause G2 arrest. Daunorubicin, an anthracycline, is known to cause DNA damage and can induce G2/M arrest by inhibiting topoisomerase II. Therefore, the option that is not a G2 phase blocker, focusing specifically on the G2 phase, is Paclitaxel, as it primarily affects the M phase rather than directly blocking the G2 phase. Daunorubicin, while it can induce G2/M arrest, is more specifically associated with G2/M phase arrest rather than G2 phase arrest alone. Thus, the most accurate answer to the question \"All of these are G2 phase blockers except:\" would be Paclitaxel, as it primarily affects the M phase rather than directly blocking the G2 phase.''' \n", "vote_history": [{"Cell Biology": "no", "Molecular Biology": "yes", "Oncology": "no", "Pharmacology": "yes", "Biochemistry": "yes", "Antimetabolites and Topoisomerase Inhibitors": "no", "Microtubule Inhibitors and Anthracyclines": "no"}, {"Cell Biology": "yes", "Molecular Biology": "yes", "Oncology": "yes", "Pharmacology": "yes", "Biochemistry": "yes", "Antimetabolites and Topoisomerase Inhibitors": "yes", "Microtubule Inhibitors and Anthracyclines": "yes"}], "revision_history": [{"Cell Biology": "Revisions: The reports collectively highlight the mechanisms and substances that act as G2 phase blockers, emphasizing their role in cell cycle regulation and ensuring genomic integrity. Among the provided options (Etoposide, Topotecan, Paclitaxel, Daunorubicin), Etoposide and Topotecan are clearly identified as G2 phase blockers due to their ability to induce DNA damage and activate the G2/M checkpoint. Paclitaxel, while it causes cell cycle arrest, primarily targets the mitotic phase (M phase) by stabilizing microtubules, though it can indirectly cause G2 arrest. Daunorubicin, an anth