 of Transient Tachypnea of the Newborn (TTN) is the presence of ground-glass opacities, which are fluffy and hazy areas on chest X-rays, primarily located in the perihilar and lower lobes of the lungs. These opacities are indicative of retained fetal lung fluid and are transient, resolving within 24 to 72 hours as the fluid is absorbed. Among the given options, the closest characteristic radiological feature is \"Air bronchogram\" (Option D), as it can be seen in the context of ground-glass opacities where the air-filled bronchi are more clearly visible against the background of retained fluid. However, it is important to note that the presence of air bronchograms is not exclusive to TTN and can be seen in other conditions as well. The radiological findings should be correlated with clinical symptoms such as rapid breathing, grunting, and nasal flaring to confirm the diagnosis of TTN. The characteristic ground-glass opacities help differentiate TTN from other respiratory conditions in newborns, ensuring appropriate management and reassurance for both clinicians and parents. Additionally, \"Reticulogranular appearance\" (Option A), \"Low volume lungs\" (Option B), and \"Prominent horizontal fissure\" (Option C) are not typically associated with TTN. The presence of air bronchograms in the context of ground-glass opacities is the key radiological feature that supports the diagnosis of TTN.''' \n", "vote_history": [{"Neonatology": "no", "Radiology": "no", "Pediatrics": "no", "Respiratory Medicine": "no", "Obstetrics": "no"}, {"Neonatology": "no", "Radiology": "no", "Pediatrics": "no", "Respiratory Medicine": "no", "Obstetrics": "no"}, {"Neonatology": "no", "Radiology": "no", "Pediatrics": "no", "Respiratory Medicine": "no", "Obstetrics": "no"}], "revision_history": [{"Neonatology": "Revisions: The radiological hallmark of Transient Tachypnea of the Newborn (TTN) is the presence of ground-glass opacities, which are fluffy and hazy areas on chest X-rays, primarily locate